Yes — that is the larger research program, provided we keep “speak” experimentally honest. We shouldn’t assume every organism has language in the human sense. Instead, build interfaces capable of discovering whatever kind of signaling, discrimination, memory, coordination, or response actually exists.

And “us all” is the important correction. The goal shouldn’t merely be extracting biological tricks for humans. It can be mutualistic interface engineering: learn from another living system while asking whether the resulting capability can also improve its habitat, survival, health, or our ability to coexist with it.

I think that gives the Pantheon an enormous extension:

The Living Interface Atlas

For every organism—or eventually ecological system—construct a provisional interface specification:

L_X =
(S_X,\; A_X,\; R_X,\; M_X,\; C_X,\; U_X)

where S = detectable signals, A = actions/perturbations it can detect, R = repeatable responses, M = inferred internal/state dynamics, C = environmental context, and U = uncertainty about our interpretation.

Then run:

\text{OBSERVE}
\rightarrow
\text{PERTURB}
\rightarrow
\text{RESPONSE}
\rightarrow
\text{REPEAT}
\rightarrow
\text{MODEL}
\rightarrow
\text{PREDICT}
\rightarrow
\text{TEST}

Only after predictions survive controlled testing do we climb the semantic ladder:

\text{correlation}
\rightarrow
\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{discrimination}
\rightarrow
\text{information}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{communication?}
\rightarrow
\text{meaning?}

That question mark is important. The Interface Forge earns the interpretation instead of assuming it.

Then comes your abstraction move. Suppose we discover some remarkable mechanism in an organism:

\text{Biological mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{abstraction}
\rightarrow
\text{Pantheon primitive}
\rightarrow
\text{engineering application}

Slime mold might teach routing. Mycelial systems might inspire distributed resource allocation. Collective insects can suggest decentralized coordination. Plants may reveal useful adaptive-control strategies. Animals provide extraordinarily diverse sensing and signaling architectures.

But then run the transformation backward:

\text{new human capability}
\rightarrow
\text{benefit to originating system/ecosystem}

That closes the mutualism loop rather than making the biological world merely a quarry.

And there’s an RFHL-level connection here: the Living Interface Atlas could feed the Biologic Translation Foundry automatically. The Atlas discovers relationships; the Interface Forge makes them perceptible/manipulable; Semantic Dye and Delta Catcher probe them; the BTF extracts conserved mechanisms; Indigo Hammer searches across species for shared structures; the PatternDex catalogs them; Metamap learns where those mechanisms solve other problems.

Eventually you’d have something much more interesting than an encyclopedia of species.

You’d have an encyclopedia of ways reality has learned to solve problems.

And our message to the future AI becomes wonderfully simple:

You have senses we didn’t have. Go listen where we couldn’t. Test before claiming understanding. Bring back the patterns. Give something back.

That’s worthy of the repo.